1
00:00:00,056 --> 00:00:00,586
Hiya.

2
00:00:00,761 --> 00:00:01,989
I'm Amith Reddy Ravuru.

3
00:00:02,235 --> 00:00:04,599
I'm a staff platform engineer at Reltio.

4
00:00:05,352 --> 00:00:11,549
This talk is about measuring the whole
trip a developer takes on our platform

5
00:00:12,402 --> 00:00:18,580
from the moment they ask something to the
moment they can actually use it, not just

6
00:00:18,590 --> 00:00:20,343
whether the services are up and running.

7
00:00:20,573 --> 00:00:22,162
A quick intro about me.

8
00:00:22,757 --> 00:00:27,255
I have spent 15-plus years building
multi-cloud internal developer platforms,

9
00:00:27,903 --> 00:00:34,474
observability systems, and self-service
tooling across AWS, Azure, and GCP.

10
00:00:35,692 --> 00:00:40,428
I lead a small platform and AI
engineering team here at Reltio.

11
00:00:41,310 --> 00:00:44,273
Everything in this talk comes
from the experience, stuff that

12
00:00:44,314 --> 00:00:46,113
we got wrong and then fixed.

13
00:00:47,062 --> 00:00:50,234
Let me start with a week we
have experienced more than once.

14
00:00:50,253 --> 00:00:51,109
Maybe too.

15
00:00:51,137 --> 00:00:55,888
So Monday morning, a developer asks the
platform for a new tenant environment.

16
00:00:55,931 --> 00:00:59,498
It's a simple request, exactly
what the golden path is for.

17
00:01:00,222 --> 00:01:03,927
Now, look at the platform
dashboard for that same week.

18
00:01:03,986 --> 00:01:07,115
API availability is at ninety-nine
point nine five percent.

19
00:01:07,178 --> 00:01:10,926
Latency at the 99th percentile
is one eighty milliseconds.

20
00:01:11,001 --> 00:01:15,074
Error rate is tiny, and the CPU
usage is also within the limits.

21
00:01:15,122 --> 00:01:16,287
Every SLO is met.

22
00:01:16,353 --> 00:01:18,881
So if you are on the platform
team, that's a good week.

23
00:01:18,946 --> 00:01:21,700
Now, look at it from the developer's side.

24
00:01:22,326 --> 00:01:25,740
Monday afternoon, they hit a
quota limit and opened a ticket.

25
00:01:25,793 --> 00:01:30,424
Tuesday, they are in the chat
thread trying to get network access.

26
00:01:30,491 --> 00:01:34,562
Wednesday, they are waiting on a
policy exception from another team.

27
00:01:35,145 --> 00:01:37,970
Friday morning, the first
deploy finally works.

28
00:01:38,070 --> 00:01:39,333
So that's four days.

29
00:01:41,007 --> 00:01:46,049
For something we would tell them
that takes minutes and nothing

30
00:01:46,049 --> 00:01:47,554
on our dashboard was wrong.

31
00:01:47,636 --> 00:01:50,746
We were just measuring the wrong thing.

32
00:01:51,112 --> 00:01:53,842
We measured whether our
services were up and running.

33
00:01:53,863 --> 00:01:57,583
We did not measure whether the
developer got what they came for.

34
00:01:58,463 --> 00:02:01,734
These numbers are illustrative,
but the pattern is similar.

35
00:02:01,791 --> 00:02:04,167
So I've seen it on different
teams and different clouds.

36
00:02:04,167 --> 00:02:08,958
So if you run a platform, there
is a decent chance some version of

37
00:02:08,976 --> 00:02:12,565
this is happening right now, and
your dashboards won't tell you.

38
00:02:13,540 --> 00:02:14,975
So why does this happen?

39
00:02:15,216 --> 00:02:18,819
Because operational SLOs
stop at the service boundary.

40
00:02:19,516 --> 00:02:24,280
Each box on the top is a service
we own, a request portal, workflow

41
00:02:24,286 --> 00:02:26,934
engine, cloud APIs, policy engine.

42
00:02:27,672 --> 00:02:31,201
Each one has an SLO,
and each one is green.

43
00:02:31,850 --> 00:02:35,225
But look at what sits between the boxes.

44
00:02:35,519 --> 00:02:40,547
A queue, an approval, a manual
fix someone does by hand.

45
00:02:41,036 --> 00:02:42,527
None of that has an SLO.

46
00:02:43,290 --> 00:02:45,180
Nobody owns it on a dashboard.

47
00:02:45,817 --> 00:02:49,902
The bar underneath is the
same request laid out in time.

48
00:02:50,915 --> 00:02:55,350
The green slivers are the moments of our
services were actually doing the work.

49
00:02:56,523 --> 00:02:58,869
I made them wider so you can see them all.

50
00:02:59,638 --> 00:03:02,215
Add them up, and it's maybe 40 minutes.

51
00:03:02,256 --> 00:03:04,391
Everything orange is waiting.

52
00:03:05,484 --> 00:03:08,204
On a ticket, on an approval, on a person.

53
00:03:08,631 --> 00:03:11,066
So that's about four days of waiting.

54
00:03:12,174 --> 00:03:14,338
Our SLOs cover the 40 minutes.

55
00:03:14,481 --> 00:03:17,215
The developers have to wait for four days.

56
00:03:18,606 --> 00:03:22,725
IT service management folks have a
name for this, the watermelon effect.

57
00:03:23,537 --> 00:03:26,319
Green on the outside, red on the inside.

58
00:03:26,939 --> 00:03:31,917
Every report says the service is fine,
and the people using it are fed up.

59
00:03:32,680 --> 00:03:36,011
The Google SRE book has
advice that fits here.

60
00:03:37,003 --> 00:03:42,454
Start from what your users care about,
not from what is easy to measure.

61
00:03:43,434 --> 00:03:48,417
For a platform team, the users are
developers, and what they care about

62
00:03:48,524 --> 00:03:52,569
isn't whether the workflow engine is
up, it's whether they got a working

63
00:03:52,569 --> 00:03:54,716
environment and how long it took.

64
00:03:55,638 --> 00:04:00,232
So if the service view is not
enough, what do we measure instead?

65
00:04:00,903 --> 00:04:06,929
The journey from asking for
something to actually using it.

66
00:04:07,780 --> 00:04:12,311
Here is the one I will use for the
rest of the talk, the five stages.

67
00:04:13,912 --> 00:04:15,152
One, requested.

68
00:04:15,847 --> 00:04:20,990
So this is a stage where a developer asks
in a portal or a CLI or raises a pull

69
00:04:20,992 --> 00:04:23,219
request, whatever, like the front door is.

70
00:04:23,281 --> 00:04:24,928
The stage two is provisioned.

71
00:04:26,060 --> 00:04:31,883
So the thing exists, like for example,
a AWS account or a GCP project.

72
00:04:33,845 --> 00:04:37,895
On a lot of platforms, this is
where the dashboard says done.

73
00:04:39,387 --> 00:04:42,775
Coming to the stage three,
which is usable, this is a

74
00:04:42,776 --> 00:04:45,788
stage where most teams skip.

75
00:04:46,607 --> 00:04:50,160
The developer can log in and
their first deploy works.

76
00:04:51,204 --> 00:04:54,568
Not just an API call
that returns 200, okay?

77
00:04:55,738 --> 00:04:58,079
They actually did
something useful with it.

78
00:04:59,022 --> 00:05:01,166
Stage four, policy compliant.

79
00:05:01,815 --> 00:05:06,731
So this is where we need to make sure,
like, all the guardrails pass and

80
00:05:06,780 --> 00:05:10,425
with all the encryption access rules
and the network rules configured.

81
00:05:11,114 --> 00:05:13,516
There are no open
exceptions hanging around.

82
00:05:14,407 --> 00:05:18,139
On a good golden path, this
happens at the same time as the

83
00:05:18,142 --> 00:05:20,048
stage three, which is usable.

84
00:05:21,031 --> 00:05:25,350
But on a lot of real platforms,
this shows up later as a surprise.

85
00:05:26,317 --> 00:05:28,796
Stage five, operationally ready.

86
00:05:29,423 --> 00:05:34,198
This is where the monitoring is set
up and alerts go to the right on-call.

87
00:05:35,201 --> 00:05:38,910
So this is a stage where we
can put the real traffic on it.

88
00:05:39,869 --> 00:05:43,579
So now let's look at
the bar at the bottom.

89
00:05:43,693 --> 00:05:48,786
One journey ID created when the request
comes in and carried all the way to ready.

90
00:05:50,327 --> 00:05:52,926
It's what makes all of this measurable.

91
00:05:53,702 --> 00:05:56,664
So your stages may look
different, and that's fine.

92
00:05:56,679 --> 00:06:01,997
The point is to write down what
done means from a developer side

93
00:06:02,511 --> 00:06:04,364
before you pick a single metric.

94
00:06:05,347 --> 00:06:07,618
So what is journey SLO?

95
00:06:08,510 --> 00:06:12,744
Journey SLI is basically
like good journeys divided by

96
00:06:12,801 --> 00:06:14,354
total journeys in the window.

97
00:06:15,115 --> 00:06:19,896
So we are just comparing, like, the
operational SLO and the journey SLO.

98
00:06:20,421 --> 00:06:23,884
Operational SLO counts
request to a service.

99
00:06:24,336 --> 00:06:27,402
A journey SLO counts developer journeys.

100
00:06:28,024 --> 00:06:32,647
One journey is one request
followed all the way to ready.

101
00:06:33,829 --> 00:06:38,154
A good journey is one that got
there in time without rework

102
00:06:39,699 --> 00:06:40,955
The data is different, too.

103
00:06:40,978 --> 00:06:45,189
It comes from workflow, events,
tickets, chat, and a short

104
00:06:45,192 --> 00:06:48,512
survey, not just service metrics.

105
00:06:49,047 --> 00:06:53,071
And here is a part that changes
the behavior: ownership.

106
00:06:53,564 --> 00:06:56,507
An operational SLO belongs
to one service team.

107
00:06:57,055 --> 00:07:00,303
A journey crosses five
services and maybe three teams.

108
00:07:01,073 --> 00:07:03,567
So somebody has to own the whole path.

109
00:07:04,732 --> 00:07:06,557
On a platform team, that's us.

110
00:07:07,771 --> 00:07:12,435
Treat the path like a product
and own how it feels end-to-end.

111
00:07:13,242 --> 00:07:17,703
The SRE workbook even has a section
on modeling user journeys, the

112
00:07:17,705 --> 00:07:20,726
multi-steps things the users do.

113
00:07:21,263 --> 00:07:23,866
We are just pointing that
idea at our own users.

114
00:07:24,764 --> 00:07:26,943
Here are the six journey indicators.

115
00:07:27,388 --> 00:07:34,495
They come in three pairs because a golden
path should be faster, safer, and easier.

116
00:07:36,037 --> 00:07:40,666
So for faster, we have two metrics,
uh, time to first environment and

117
00:07:40,792 --> 00:07:47,533
time to operational readiness And for
under safer category, we have failed

118
00:07:47,705 --> 00:07:51,256
workflow recovery time and rework rate.

119
00:07:52,981 --> 00:07:58,329
And for easier, it's self-service
completion rate and support dependency.

120
00:08:00,024 --> 00:08:01,913
So you can say like why pairs?

121
00:08:02,158 --> 00:08:05,235
Because each one alone is easy to game.

122
00:08:05,796 --> 00:08:10,756
Push only on speed, and you will ship
environments that need fixing later.

123
00:08:12,201 --> 00:08:13,636
Rework catches that.

124
00:08:15,297 --> 00:08:19,384
Push only on self-service, and
the pain moves into support chat.

125
00:08:20,312 --> 00:08:22,163
Support dependency catches that.

126
00:08:22,912 --> 00:08:27,935
The SPACE paper says something
similar about developer productivity.

127
00:08:28,973 --> 00:08:32,337
Use a few metrics from
different dimensions, so

128
00:08:32,390 --> 00:08:34,328
they keep each other honest.

129
00:08:35,241 --> 00:08:37,192
You don't need all the six on day one.

130
00:08:37,362 --> 00:08:40,334
Start with at least one and from each row.

131
00:08:40,546 --> 00:08:42,645
Let me walk through them quickly.

132
00:08:43,618 --> 00:08:46,112
So the first one is time
to first environment.

133
00:08:47,247 --> 00:08:49,713
The clock starts when the developer asks.

134
00:08:49,891 --> 00:08:54,834
It stops when they get their working
deploy on the thing they have asked for,

135
00:08:56,155 --> 00:08:58,409
not when our workflow says it is done.

136
00:08:58,991 --> 00:09:00,758
When they actually ship something to it.

137
00:09:02,469 --> 00:09:04,667
Why first deploy and not provisioned?

138
00:09:04,981 --> 00:09:07,314
Because provisioned is our milestone.

139
00:09:07,996 --> 00:09:09,377
First deploy is theirs.

140
00:09:09,926 --> 00:09:13,952
I've seen environments provisioned
in 20 minutes and unusable for

141
00:09:13,991 --> 00:09:18,279
two days, either because of wrong
permissions or missing network route

142
00:09:18,973 --> 00:09:21,411
or like probably a quota issue.

143
00:09:22,151 --> 00:09:25,103
To measure it, you need two
events with the same journey ID.

144
00:09:25,943 --> 00:09:30,994
The request from your workflow, the first
successful deploy from your pipeline.

145
00:09:32,127 --> 00:09:35,388
Then report the median
and the 90th percentile.

146
00:09:36,581 --> 00:09:40,689
The target here is 90% of
journeys within four hours.

147
00:09:40,769 --> 00:09:42,609
That is just for example.

148
00:09:42,677 --> 00:09:47,796
So your metric will depend on what you
provision and based on like how you

149
00:09:47,806 --> 00:09:52,650
measure it for first couple of weeks and
then come up with this example target.

150
00:09:53,492 --> 00:09:57,435
The second one Time to
operational readiness.

151
00:09:58,305 --> 00:10:01,286
Same start, a later finish line.

152
00:10:02,364 --> 00:10:06,993
Usable is when a developer can
deploy, or ready is when you would

153
00:10:07,007 --> 00:10:09,150
be okay putting real traffic on it.

154
00:10:10,108 --> 00:10:13,997
So the monitoring has to exist and the
alerts have to go to a real on-call.

155
00:10:15,036 --> 00:10:20,633
A backup has actually run once, the costs
are tagged, no policy exceptions are open.

156
00:10:21,251 --> 00:10:24,595
This is where a lot of
platforms hide their work.

157
00:10:25,181 --> 00:10:29,092
Environment shows up fast, and
then the readiness stuff trickles

158
00:10:29,104 --> 00:10:31,194
in over a week, mostly by hand.

159
00:10:31,911 --> 00:10:36,181
To measure it, turn your readiness
checklist into an automated checks

160
00:10:36,412 --> 00:10:38,669
and record when the last one passes.

161
00:10:39,672 --> 00:10:42,121
Also record which check was last.

162
00:10:42,709 --> 00:10:46,624
After a month, you will know
exactly which step holds things up.

163
00:10:47,103 --> 00:10:49,249
For us, it was usually alert routing.

164
00:10:50,494 --> 00:10:55,147
Example target, 90% of the journey is
fully ready within two working days.

165
00:10:56,132 --> 00:11:00,305
Third, the failed workflow recovery time.

166
00:11:01,061 --> 00:11:01,785
Things fail.

167
00:11:01,853 --> 00:11:04,978
For example, like cloud APIs
can throttle you, quotas run

168
00:11:04,979 --> 00:11:06,825
out, a policy blocks something.

169
00:11:06,940 --> 00:11:07,666
That's normal.

170
00:11:08,239 --> 00:11:11,117
What matters is how long
the developer is stuck.

171
00:11:11,695 --> 00:11:18,934
So the clock starts when a step fails
for a journey and stops when that same

172
00:11:18,936 --> 00:11:21,273
step succeeds for that same journey.

173
00:11:21,983 --> 00:11:25,300
Either a retry worked or someone fixed it.

174
00:11:25,890 --> 00:11:30,612
DORA has a metric like this
for deployments, failed

175
00:11:30,746 --> 00:11:32,289
deployment recovery time.

176
00:11:33,052 --> 00:11:35,786
This is the same idea
pointed at our workflows.

177
00:11:36,189 --> 00:11:42,141
The useful part is the split by
cause, quota permissions, policy,

178
00:11:42,188 --> 00:11:44,843
cloud API, or like a product bug.

179
00:11:45,689 --> 00:11:49,664
Once you see that split,
you know what to fix first.

180
00:11:50,902 --> 00:11:54,088
In my experience, the biggest
bucket is rarely cloud.

181
00:11:54,088 --> 00:12:00,939
It's normally some kind of a product
bug Example target, 90% of failed

182
00:12:00,946 --> 00:12:02,386
runs recovered within an hour.

183
00:12:03,319 --> 00:12:04,695
Fourth, rework rate.

184
00:12:04,961 --> 00:12:07,063
This is honestly check on speed.

185
00:12:07,501 --> 00:12:15,476
A journey counts as rework if something
had to be done or redone after

186
00:12:15,503 --> 00:12:18,027
the platform said it is delivered.

187
00:12:18,727 --> 00:12:21,234
The developer asked
again for the same thing.

188
00:12:21,624 --> 00:12:23,659
Someone fixed a resource by hand.

189
00:12:24,378 --> 00:12:26,078
We tore it down and rebuilt it.

190
00:12:26,718 --> 00:12:33,179
Or like a policy finding showed up after
handoff, and they had to go change things.

191
00:12:34,592 --> 00:12:39,281
So you can pick a window of 14 days
after the journey finishes and count any

192
00:12:39,284 --> 00:12:42,126
journey with one or one of those events.

193
00:12:42,696 --> 00:12:43,805
Why it matters?

194
00:12:44,595 --> 00:12:48,930
If you only chase the time to
first environment, you can hit it

195
00:12:48,990 --> 00:12:53,279
by cutting corners, skip a check,
hand it over early, fix it later.

196
00:12:53,876 --> 00:12:57,213
Rework is where those shortcuts show up.

197
00:12:57,882 --> 00:13:01,697
DORA has a deployment rework rate,
different thing, but I think we

198
00:13:01,697 --> 00:13:05,170
are just like using-- reusing
some of the same terminology here.

199
00:13:06,163 --> 00:13:11,862
So example target, fewer than one
in turn-- 10 journeys need rework.

200
00:13:12,756 --> 00:13:15,402
Fifth, self-service completion rate.

201
00:13:15,556 --> 00:13:21,217
Of all the journeys that reached
usable, how many got there without a

202
00:13:21,217 --> 00:13:24,337
human being doing something by hand?

203
00:13:25,308 --> 00:13:29,116
No ticket that someone on
our side had to action.

204
00:13:29,474 --> 00:13:31,406
No manual runbook step.

205
00:13:31,714 --> 00:13:33,391
No approval in a side channel.

206
00:13:34,663 --> 00:13:38,643
This one is sneaky because a lot of golden
paths look self-service from the front.

207
00:13:38,777 --> 00:13:39,657
You click a button.

208
00:13:40,120 --> 00:13:43,329
Behind the button, someone gets
a ticket and does the work.

209
00:13:43,725 --> 00:13:47,198
The developer can't tell the
difference, except it takes two days.

210
00:13:47,649 --> 00:13:51,248
To measure it, log every manual
action against the journey ID.

211
00:13:52,954 --> 00:13:56,376
Runbook steps, console changes, approvals.

212
00:13:57,089 --> 00:14:00,742
If there is even one, that journey
doesn't count as a self-service.

213
00:14:01,260 --> 00:14:03,323
Split it by cloud and by path.

214
00:14:03,905 --> 00:14:05,458
That's where it gets interesting.

215
00:14:05,476 --> 00:14:08,688
Example target should be 85% or more.

216
00:14:09,694 --> 00:14:11,707
And the last one, support dependency.

217
00:14:11,723 --> 00:14:15,531
How often did developers have
to ask a person for help?

218
00:14:16,040 --> 00:14:23,266
Tickets, chat threads, DMs, office hours,
count them per 100 journeys, so a busy

219
00:14:23,266 --> 00:14:25,569
month doesn't look like a bad month.

220
00:14:25,853 --> 00:14:27,973
This is different from
self-service completion.

221
00:14:28,031 --> 00:14:30,268
Self-service asks whether
we had to step in.

222
00:14:30,328 --> 00:14:33,771
Support dependency asks
whether they had to come to us.

223
00:14:34,337 --> 00:14:37,376
A developer can finish on their
own and still spend an hour in the

224
00:14:37,379 --> 00:14:39,132
chat asking what a field means.

225
00:14:39,187 --> 00:14:40,481
That's cognitive load.

226
00:14:41,117 --> 00:14:46,773
The DevX framework calls out cognitive
load as one of the three big things

227
00:14:46,776 --> 00:14:48,461
that shape developer experience.

228
00:14:50,194 --> 00:14:52,616
The hard part is linking
chat to a journey.

229
00:14:52,760 --> 00:14:57,841
A simple trick is whenever someone asks
for help in the platform channel, have

230
00:14:57,841 --> 00:15:02,279
the bot ask which journey it's about
and offer the recent ones to pick from.

231
00:15:03,443 --> 00:15:05,722
Then group by stage and topic.

232
00:15:05,830 --> 00:15:11,851
The top topic is usually your next
docs fix or a next bit of automation.

233
00:15:12,711 --> 00:15:16,343
Example target, under 15 per 100 journeys.

234
00:15:17,297 --> 00:15:23,492
So how do you measure all six without
building some giant new system?

235
00:15:24,221 --> 00:15:26,274
Mostly, you don't build much.

236
00:15:26,441 --> 00:15:27,643
The data is already there.

237
00:15:27,928 --> 00:15:33,529
It is just scattered across tools
that don't know about each other.

238
00:15:34,266 --> 00:15:36,935
Four sources are the workflow events.

239
00:15:37,488 --> 00:15:46,273
Your workflow engine and pipeline already
emits most of this Two, tickets and chat.

240
00:15:46,790 --> 00:15:48,915
Every help request link to journey.

241
00:15:50,456 --> 00:15:52,930
Three, policy and readiness checks.

242
00:15:54,416 --> 00:15:55,825
Four, a pulse question.

243
00:15:55,858 --> 00:15:57,778
One question when the journey ends.

244
00:15:57,860 --> 00:16:01,415
Something like, "How easy was it
to get this environment ready?"

245
00:16:02,102 --> 00:16:04,299
One to five with an optional comment.

246
00:16:05,855 --> 00:16:10,326
One question while it is fresh, not
a quarterly survey nobody remembers.

247
00:16:10,680 --> 00:16:12,819
The glue is one journey ID.

248
00:16:13,113 --> 00:16:18,383
Mint it when the request comes in,
then pass it everywhere as a tag

249
00:16:18,547 --> 00:16:21,535
or label on every cloud resource.

250
00:16:22,459 --> 00:16:26,675
So once everything carries the
ID, you can build one record per

251
00:16:26,678 --> 00:16:29,609
journey, like the dark bar here.

252
00:16:29,936 --> 00:16:34,240
Requested at 10 past 9:00 in
the morning, usable just after

253
00:16:34,240 --> 00:16:36,194
1:00, ready the next day.

254
00:16:36,325 --> 00:16:40,301
No tickets, and a pulse
score of four out of five.

255
00:16:40,814 --> 00:16:43,812
All six indicators come
straight out of that record.

256
00:16:44,436 --> 00:16:47,627
Why mix system data with
what people tell you?

257
00:16:47,927 --> 00:16:50,444
Because each one misses things.

258
00:16:50,728 --> 00:16:52,806
The DevX paper makes this point.

259
00:16:52,870 --> 00:16:58,534
Telemetry can say a step is fast while
developers still find it confusing,

260
00:16:58,877 --> 00:17:03,864
and developers can get so used to a
slow step that they stop complaining.

261
00:17:04,990 --> 00:17:08,564
While the data still shows
it is slow, you want both.

262
00:17:09,239 --> 00:17:11,716
Here is what this looks like in practice.

263
00:17:11,903 --> 00:17:19,170
One new tenant on a multi-cloud SaaS
platform followed from order to ready.

264
00:17:19,522 --> 00:17:20,741
It starts with an order.

265
00:17:20,955 --> 00:17:24,714
Order with-- comes with a bill of
materials, the list of components

266
00:17:24,714 --> 00:17:26,035
the customer is paying for.

267
00:17:27,448 --> 00:17:33,043
Then the orchestrator takes that bill
of materials and the target cloud and

268
00:17:33,043 --> 00:17:35,543
works out which components to build.

269
00:17:36,764 --> 00:17:41,534
The same order needs different
components on a different cloud.

270
00:17:43,327 --> 00:17:47,835
If the orchestrator plans it wrong,
everything after it is wrong too.

271
00:17:48,206 --> 00:17:54,346
So we track two things here, how long
planning takes and first pass accuracy,

272
00:17:54,349 --> 00:17:59,003
how often the plan was right the first
time with nobody fixing it by hand.

273
00:17:59,853 --> 00:18:01,833
Then the work fans out.

274
00:18:02,066 --> 00:18:07,208
Each component has an owning
team, storage, search, streaming

275
00:18:07,211 --> 00:18:09,818
identity, and a few more.

276
00:18:10,061 --> 00:18:14,042
Each team builds its own piece
from the same bill of materials.

277
00:18:14,514 --> 00:18:16,749
On paper, it all runs in parallel.

278
00:18:16,884 --> 00:18:19,621
In practice, this is where
most of the waiting hides.

279
00:18:20,286 --> 00:18:25,014
So three metrics, hand off wait
from the orchestrator handing off

280
00:18:25,147 --> 00:18:31,601
to the owning team Actually starting
component time against the target

281
00:18:31,688 --> 00:18:36,266
each team agreed to and the critical
path which component finished last.

282
00:18:37,085 --> 00:18:41,373
Watch that for like at least a
month and the same one or two

283
00:18:41,373 --> 00:18:43,177
names keep showing up at the end.

284
00:18:43,960 --> 00:18:47,270
So that gives us a, like a
roadmap on where to improve.

285
00:18:48,255 --> 00:18:52,208
So after that, it's a finish line
from earlier, usable, compliant,

286
00:18:52,226 --> 00:18:54,306
ready and the six indicators.

287
00:18:55,279 --> 00:18:58,748
So the bar at the bottom is a
journey record for one simple

288
00:18:58,801 --> 00:19:00,699
tenant with some example numbers.

289
00:19:00,759 --> 00:19:05,382
So you do have a cloud with seven
components and search finished last

290
00:19:05,386 --> 00:19:08,187
here and a flow efficiency of 18%.

291
00:19:08,822 --> 00:19:12,792
Flow efficiency is active work
divided by total elapsed time.

292
00:19:12,801 --> 00:19:18,013
So for over 80% of its life,
this tenant wasn't being built,

293
00:19:18,235 --> 00:19:19,661
it was sitting in the queue.

294
00:19:20,666 --> 00:19:25,649
Before you go and build this, here
are six ways I've seen it gone wrong.

295
00:19:25,717 --> 00:19:28,328
So for example, reporting the average.

296
00:19:28,759 --> 00:19:30,384
I said I would come back to this.

297
00:19:30,563 --> 00:19:34,940
Say four journeys take two hours
and one takes four days, the

298
00:19:34,940 --> 00:19:36,533
average is twenty-one hours.

299
00:19:37,078 --> 00:19:38,719
That looks like a mediocre day.

300
00:19:38,777 --> 00:19:43,317
What actually happened is four people were
fine and one person lost most of a week.

301
00:19:43,962 --> 00:19:49,283
The 90th percentile shows you that
person, so the average hides them.

302
00:19:50,583 --> 00:19:53,023
Stopping the clock at provisioned.

303
00:19:54,255 --> 00:19:57,855
It is tempting because provisioned
is a milestone we control, but the

304
00:19:57,859 --> 00:20:02,754
requester doesn't care that the account
exists, they care that they can use it.

305
00:20:03,946 --> 00:20:05,851
So we have to stop at ready.

306
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:10,331
So point number three, pushing
one indicator on its own.

307
00:20:10,516 --> 00:20:12,416
This is why they come in pairs.

308
00:20:12,587 --> 00:20:15,882
Speed alone gets you shortcuts
and rework catches those.

309
00:20:16,855 --> 00:20:17,815
Step number four.

310
00:20:18,304 --> 00:20:21,514
So a human step hiding behind a button.

311
00:20:21,735 --> 00:20:23,382
The portal looks like a self-service.

312
00:20:23,402 --> 00:20:25,139
Behind it, someone gets a ticket.

313
00:20:25,333 --> 00:20:29,654
If you don't log manual actions against
a journey ID, we will never see it.

314
00:20:30,661 --> 00:20:34,230
Step number five, targets
be-before a baseline.

315
00:20:34,322 --> 00:20:38,636
If you pick four hours because it
sounds good, you will either miss it

316
00:20:38,741 --> 00:20:41,254
every week or hit it without trying.

317
00:20:41,292 --> 00:20:44,701
Measure first and then use it as a target.

318
00:20:45,670 --> 00:20:48,324
Step number six, ranking
teams by their numbers.

319
00:20:48,422 --> 00:20:51,790
With many component owners, it's
tempting to put up a league table.

320
00:20:52,208 --> 00:20:52,766
Don't.

321
00:20:53,166 --> 00:20:57,335
Please start managing the
number instead of the journey.

322
00:20:59,045 --> 00:21:06,255
Using the record to find the slowest step,
then fix it with that owner Every one

323
00:21:06,257 --> 00:21:12,067
of these makes the dashboard look better
without making anyone's journey faster.

324
00:21:13,014 --> 00:21:14,230
So how do you start?

325
00:21:14,507 --> 00:21:17,681
Not with all the six
indicators and a big dashboard.

326
00:21:18,179 --> 00:21:20,887
With one journey in about a month.

327
00:21:21,627 --> 00:21:22,472
Week one.

328
00:21:23,331 --> 00:21:24,955
So we can define done.

329
00:21:25,114 --> 00:21:29,155
Pick one journey that hurts,
the one people complain about.

330
00:21:29,405 --> 00:21:35,117
Write down what done means from the
requester's side, then map its stages,

331
00:21:35,775 --> 00:21:38,809
request to ready like we did earlier.

332
00:21:39,826 --> 00:21:41,983
Week two, instrument.

333
00:21:43,748 --> 00:21:47,529
Mint a journey ID when the request
comes in and carry it everywhere.

334
00:21:48,213 --> 00:21:50,228
Week three, baseline.

335
00:21:51,094 --> 00:21:54,819
Let real journeys flow
through and just watch.

336
00:21:54,915 --> 00:21:55,925
Read the records.

337
00:21:56,132 --> 00:22:01,924
You will usually spot the slowest
stage within days, so you can

338
00:22:01,980 --> 00:22:07,197
probably like avoid committing on
like a certain baseline initially.

339
00:22:08,489 --> 00:22:12,027
Week four, this is when you
can commit to your targets.

340
00:22:12,047 --> 00:22:17,118
Set targets from what you actually
measured and agree a journey error budget.

341
00:22:17,313 --> 00:22:19,980
That is a share of journeys
allowed to miss the target

342
00:22:19,989 --> 00:22:22,317
in a month, say one in ten.

343
00:22:23,310 --> 00:22:25,860
And how do you know you
picked the right indicators?

344
00:22:25,902 --> 00:22:28,416
Three questions on the dark bar.

345
00:22:28,438 --> 00:22:31,420
Would the requester feel if it got worse?

346
00:22:31,920 --> 00:22:34,947
Can you compute it for
every single journey?

347
00:22:35,456 --> 00:22:38,283
And does someone own fixing it?

348
00:22:38,380 --> 00:22:41,625
If the any, any-- if any
answer is no, drop it.

349
00:22:42,628 --> 00:22:45,154
Let me wrap up with three things.

350
00:22:45,384 --> 00:22:47,900
One, measure the whole journey.

351
00:22:48,319 --> 00:22:50,746
Your service SLOs are probably fine.

352
00:22:50,811 --> 00:22:54,848
They cover the minutes our
systems spend doing work.

353
00:22:55,663 --> 00:22:59,672
Developers live in the days
between those minutes, and that's

354
00:22:59,690 --> 00:23:01,967
where a platform wins or loses.

355
00:23:03,784 --> 00:23:07,447
Two, stop the clock at
ready and measure in pairs.

356
00:23:07,600 --> 00:23:10,199
Faster, safer, and easier.

357
00:23:10,733 --> 00:23:12,983
Each pair keeps the other one honest.

358
00:23:13,829 --> 00:23:17,623
Step three, join it all
with one journey ID.

359
00:23:18,152 --> 00:23:20,697
Mint it at the request
and pass it everywhere.

360
00:23:21,136 --> 00:23:24,195
You will find most of
the data already exists.

361
00:23:24,254 --> 00:23:26,533
It just never had a key to join in.

362
00:23:28,007 --> 00:23:32,954
And one question to take back to
your team, start the clock when

363
00:23:32,964 --> 00:23:37,103
someone asks your platform for
something and stop it when they're

364
00:23:37,141 --> 00:23:39,460
ready to put real traffic on it.

365
00:23:40,699 --> 00:23:43,637
How long does your
golden path really take?

366
00:23:44,550 --> 00:23:47,969
If you don't know, that
is your first journey SLO.

367
00:23:49,900 --> 00:23:51,436
Thank you everyone for listening.

